 "sections": [
  {
   "id": "what",
   "h2": "A tense for the past",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "lead": "The <b>imperfect</b> tense describes something that was going on in the past, or that used to happen again and again. English usually needs two or three words for it: \"was carrying\", \"used to carry\"."
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "servus cibum porta<u class=\"c\">ba</u><u class=\"v\">t</u>.",
      "en": "The slave <b>was carrying</b> food.",
      "note": "<em>-ba-</em> marks the imperfect, and <em>-t</em> is still \"he\" or \"she\"."
     }
    },
    {
     "facts": [
      "The sign of the imperfect is <em>-ba-</em>, between the stem and the person ending.",
      "The person endings are the ones you know, except that \"I\" ends in <em>-m</em>: <em>portabam</em>.",
      "Translate it \"was ...ing\" or \"were ...ing\" for something going on, and \"used to ...\" for a habit."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
